  "narrative": "No assessments are due at this explore stage. I registered 12 new comparisons to use the remaining discovery budget productively. The main strategic priority is not to chase sparse or weak interaction claims, because independent validation is exhausted and many interaction contrasts have been unresolved or rejected. Instead, these proposals test whether the strongest dataset-only observational associations persist in important demographic and biological strata, and whether two additional mechanistically motivated main effects exist outside the already supported predictor-outcome pairs. For the accepted association pattern, direction is retained: direction -1 is used when the hypothesized exposed group has stronger dependency, meaning raw exposed-minus-comparator dependency is more negative; direction +1 is used when the hypothesized exposed group has weaker dependency, meaning raw exposed-minus-comparator dependency is less positive or less negative. Returned estimates will already be signed to the registered direction, so positive values support the registered hypothesis and negative values oppose it. Sex-specific strata for SMARCA2-SMARCA4, WRN-MSI-high, STAT3-PTEN loss, USP7-TP53 loss, MDM2-CDKN2A loss in TP53-intact cells, and NFE2L2-KEAP1 loss test whether these large effects are robust when evaluated in female cells, not only as artifacts of the male-dominant population or previously tested male strata. The high-stemness PTEN-STAT3 stratum tests whether the PTEN-loss effect remains in a de-differentiated state, which is biologically relevant but could otherwise be confounded by cellular-state heterogeneity. The TP53-loss USP7 stratum within PTEN-loss cells tests whether the TP53-USP7 association persists when a second major tumor-suppressor pathway is altered; a near-null or opposite result would suggest co-pathway buffering or confounding, while a large positive signed estimate would support persistence. The MYC-amplified, TP53-intact MDM2 stratum tests whether the CDKN2A-loss-MDM2 association remains in a proliferative/transcriptionally amplified context. The two new PTEN-loss main-effect claims for POLR2A and RPL11 test a different mechanistic axis: PTEN loss may increase PI3K/AKT/mTOR-associated biosynthetic demand, potentially strengthening dependency on RNA polymerase II and ribosome-biogenesis machinery. The POLR2A MYC-amplification test in low baseline MYC-expression cells tests whether MYC amplification has context-dependent transcriptional effects when baseline MYC mRNA is low; if MYC amplification is buffered or nonfunctional in this setting, the estimate should be near null or opposite. For outcome-scale importance, I will judge signed lower confidence bounds against approximately 10% of the absolute mean dependency score: SMARCA2 about 0.042, WRN about 0.030, STAT3 about 0.028, MDM2 about 0.027, POLR2A about 0.025, RPL11 about 0.025, and lower but still relevant scales for USP7 and NFE2L2. Because no voluntary validation budget remains, newly discovered effects must be judged from discovery uncertainty, cell sizes, consistency with existing accepted effects, and plausibility, with extra caution for new exploratory axes. All conclusions remain observational associations in the supplied dataset only, not causal effects or claims about generalizability."
